title: Platform open questions
tags: Platform
kind: question
status: deferred
summary: Unicode normalisation of names, Windows long paths, and what a port to macOS or Windows would take.

* ''Unicode normalisation.'' macOS filesystems normalise names (HFS+ stores NFD; APFS compares normalisation-insensitively). A repository created on Linux with NFC names and opened on a Mac may see the "same" name as another byte sequence, which a lookup by bytes misses. Whether names should be normalised (to NFC) at the filesystem boundary, or compared normalisation-insensitively the way case is ([[Case sensitivity]]), is open — normalising breaks the rule that the stored name is the exact bytes ([[Tree names]]).
* ''Windows long paths.'' A path longer than 260 characters needs the `\\?\` prefix or a registry opt-in; whether the daemon should add the prefix itself is open.
* ''Porting: to be built'' (user, 27 November 2026: Windows at least, macOS preferably; the steps are in `roadmap.org`). The daemon calls `flock`, `statvfs`, `sysconf` and Unix sockets without a fallback ([[Platform]]); Windows needs `LockFileEx` for [[The repository lock]], `GetDiskFreeSpaceEx` for the store's growth, and another transport to the fanotify broker (or none: it is Linux-only). The GUI needs another way to confine its media decoders than `bwrap` ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]), and the session tokens their hardening ([[Session tokens]]).
* ''Path arguments that are not UTF-8: given up'' (user, 27 November 2026). The clients that move files now take a path from the daemon as its exact bytes, and a repository path crosses the API as `{text, bytes}` when it is not text ([[Tree names]]). A ''native'' path argument stays a JSON string, so `mf` refuses one that is not UTF-8 — `mf repo init`/`load`, `mf metarecord add --path`, `mf uuid`, `mf trash -f`, `mf order` ([[Path representation]]). Such a file is still tracked by reconcile and the watcher; naming it on the command line was never asked for, and would have meant `{text, bytes}` on each of those endpoints and a new API version.
